  "dry_snp_activity": "This week saw heightened activity across most dry segments, led by strong appetite for modern tonnage. On the Newcastlemax front, the “Mineral Shougang International” – 206K/2019 Qingdao was sold to Chi- nese buyers Zhenjiang Shipping for USD 65.5 mills, a level reflecting both her young age and scrubber-fitted status. In the Kamsarmax segment, two sister units, the “SDTR Doris” and “SDTR Celeste” – 85K/2021 Shanhaiguan, were sold enbloc to undisclosed buyers for USD 55 mills, highlighting continued demand for modern Chinese-built eco tonnage. The 2019-built “Duke Santos” – 82K Jiangsu Newyangzi changed hands for low/mid USD 27 mills, while the 2016-built “Nord Crux” – 82K Tsuneishi Cebu achieved in excess of USD 26.4 mills, both to undisclosed interests. Older Kamsarmax units also saw movement, with the “Montana I” – 82K/2011 Daewoo sold at USD 15.4 mills basis SS/DD due. In the Panamax size, the “Atheras” – 74K/2006 Hudong-Zhonghua fetched mid USD 8 mills, while the Japanese-built “Navios Helios” – 77K/2005 Namura was sold for USD 8.25 mills, both to undisclosed buyers. On the Supramax side, the “Haut Brion” – 57K/2011 Taizhou Sanfu was sold to Chinese buyers for USD 12.3 mills, and the “Icarius” – 56K/2007 Mitsui fetched USD 11.75 mills, also to Chinese interests. Activity remained firm in the Handysize segment as well, with the Japanese- built “TBC Prime” – 39K/2011 Minaminippon sold to Vietnamese buyers for USD 14 mills basis OHBS, while the “Bright Hope” – 28K/2010 Imabari achieved high USD 9 mills. Finally, the 2005-built “CSE Clipper Express” – 28K Ima- bari was sold for USD 5.5 mills, rounding off a busy week across the dry market.",
  "wet_snp_activity": "Activity in the tanker market remained steady this week. In the VLCC segment, the “Dalma” – 306K/2007 Daewoo was sold to Chinese buyers for USD 48 mills, reflecting firm pricing for older scrubber-fitted Korean tonnage. On the Suezmax sector, the “Brightway” – 160K/2012 HHIC changed hands for USD 44.5 mills, while UAE-based Safeen acquired enbloc the Aframax sister ships “STI Lavender” and “STI Lobelia” – both 110K/2019 New Times, for a total price of USD 122.4 mills enbloc. The pair are scrubber-fitted and coated, highlighting continued appetite for modern eco ton- nage. On the MR2 front, the “Green Sky” – 51K/2014 Dae Sun was reported sold to Greek buyers for USD 30.5 mills. Further down the size spectrum, the “Falcon Royal” – 47K/2008 HMD fetched mid USD 16 mills, while the “Maritime Jingan” – 44K/2003 Dalian achieved USD 9 mills, rounding off a relatively active week across the clean and dirty tanker segments.",
